(a) A finding of noncompliance with any building, fire, health, sanitation or life safety code, regulation or ordinance now or hereafter adopted by the mayor and city council shall constitute prima facie evidence that a public nuisance exists. Any written citation served upon a property owner by a public officer charged with code enforcement shall specify the code section deemed to be violated and state sufficient details to apprise the property owner of the basis for citation.
(b) The public officer charged with code enforcement shall have the following powers and duties:
(1) To investigate the conditions existing on any private property within the city to determine which dwellings, buildings or structures are unfit for human habitation or for commercial, industrial or business use; provided, however, that no public officer shall enter into a secured structure without consent of the owner or tenant in possession unless he first obtains a warrant based upon probable cause.
(2) To delegate any of his functions and powers under this article to his subordinates as he deems necessary to carry out the purpose of this article.
(c) In addition to specified code violations noticed and without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the following conditions are subject to abatement:
(1) Defects increasing the hazards of fire, accidents or other calamities, including improper storage of materials on the premises;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation and disrepair, including those structures which have been damaged by fire, wind or other causes such that 35 percent or more of the external structure and roof have been destroyed or structural members weakened;
(4) Sanitation and general uncleanliness, including improper keeping of pets and animals on the premises, and improper disposal of human and animal wastes;
(5) Accumulations of trash, weeds (having a height of 12 inches or more), debris, junk, filth, standing or stagnant water and other unsafe conditions; and
(6) Vacant, unsecured dwellings, buildings or structures in which drug crimes are being committed.
(d) Signs in public right-of-way. Pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 32-6-51, any sign erected, placed, or maintained within the dedicated right-of-way of any public road, except as expressly authorized by law, shall be considered a public nuisance, and may be removed by the city, or directed to be removed.
(Ord. No. 96-11, art. I(D), 5-7-1996; Ord. No. 03-12-2007, § 1, 12-27-2007)

562:
It shall be unlawful for any person to create or cause to be created a disturbance which interferes with peace, tranquility and good order of the city. It shall further be unlawful for any person in possession and control of premises within the city to allow other persons located upon the premises to violate this article.
Sound measurement standards for the City of Jackson and for the purpose of this article "plainly audible" shall mean any sound emanating from a specific sound-producing source which can be heard from the distances set forth in this section, using the following sound measurement standards: measurement shall be by the auditory senses of a person standing at a distance no less than the required minimum distance from the source of its sound. Restrictions of 100 feet for 7:00 p.m. through 11:00 a.m. Sunday through Saturday.
(1) Except when noise emanates from a source exempted by this article, it is a violation of this article for any person or persons to sing, yell, shout, hoot, whistle, or play a musical instrument, on the public streets or sidewalks or on private property so as to create, or cause to be created, any noises or sounds which are plainly audible at a distance of 100 feet or more from the building, structure, or vehicle from which the sound is emanating, or when generated outdoors on real property, 100 feet beyond the boundary or property limits on which the person is located, between the hours of 7:00 p.m. and 11:00 a.m. Sunday through Saturday.
(2) Except for persons operating within properly licensed or permitted non-residential enterprises that have an adjoining property line or boundary with a residential dwelling unit, during the days and hours set forth in this section, it is a violation of this article for any person to make, cause, or continue to make any noise in such a manner as to be plainly audible to any other person a distance of five feet beyond the adjoining wall or boundary of any apartment, condominium, townhouse, duplex, or other such residential dwelling units with adjoining points of contact. For the purposes of this section, "wall or boundary" shall mean an imaginary line drawn through the points of contact of (1) adjoining apartments, condominiums, townhouses, duplexes or other such residential dwelling units with adjoining points owned, rented, or leased by different persons; or (2) adjoining common areas or adjoining exterior walls. Said property line or boundary includes all points of a plane formed by projecting the property line or boundary including the ceiling, the floor, and the walls.
The prohibitions of this article shall not apply to the following sources of noise:
(1) Noises and/or sounds caused to be made by manufacturing, industrial, governmental, or commercial entities in the normal course of their business;
(2) Noises and/or sounds emanating from any official event on property owned by a federal, state, or local government, or any official event on Butts County School District property, Butts County property, or City of Jackson property;
(3) Noises and/or sounds that are permitted by a license or permit issued by the city; provided, however, that the producer or coordinator of the event must strictly comply with the terms, restrictions and conditions of the permit;
(4) Noises or sounds made by birds and animals;
(5) Noises or sounds normally made by cars, trucks, trains, airplanes, helicopters, buses and similar vehicles, when properly equipped with noise-limiting devices required by law;
(6) Sound volumes produced by radio, CD or tape player, or other mechanical sound making device or instrument from within a motor vehicle while on a public street or highway, which sound is controlled by the O.C.G.A. § 40-6-14;
(7) Noises or sounds from property maintenance activity conducted on or within 100 feet of any residential dwelling unit between the hours of 8:00 a.m. and 8:00 p.m.;
(8) Noises or sounds from construction activities conducted on or within 100 feet of a property zoned for single-family or multi-family residential use between the hours of 6:30 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. (does not apply within commercial classifications);
(9) Noises or sounds made by law enforcement and other public safety officials performing their public functions; or
(10) The prohibitions of this section shall not apply to government road, water, sewer, stormwater construction or maintenance projects or to Georgia Department of Transportation Road construction or maintenance projects.
(Ord. No. 94-5, § 3, 6-21-1994; Ord. No. 96-11A, 8-21-2001; Ord. No. 2025-004, 8-19-2025)

